  "designIntent": {
    "firstRead": "A low rear-wrapping maid bonnet and a blue cloth dress with a narrow asymmetric white apron, not Lucario plus armor plates.",
    "macroRules": [
      "No stacked horizontal cap slabs.",
      "No large rectangular bonnet plates beside the ears.",
      "No single rectangular apron board.",
      "No paired rectangular apron shields.",
      "No rigid folding-fan silhouette across the whole front.",
      "Keep the white apron subordinate to the blue skirt silhouette.",
      "Build the dress silhouette through side and rear drapes, not by inflating a frontal white mass.",
      "The bonnet lives over and behind the head between/outside the ear roots; the forehead and face stay open.",
      "Use very thin surfaces for cloth and reserve volumetric cuboids for actual volume.",
      "Keep both ears and the face visually open.",
      "Carry blue/periwinkle cloth through torso, sleeves, hips, front, side and rear; use white as trim/apron hierarchy.",
      "Spend geometry on contour changes, not repeated identical blocks."
    ],
    "referenceTechniqueCarryover": [
      "Two-stage parented drape and edge hierarchy from the inspected Ruins Style technique study.",
      "Whole-body costume continuity and anatomy-following shells from the inspected Space Style technique study.",
      "Connected head/torso garment hierarchy and animation-parented overlays from the inspected Covert Style technique study."
    ],
    "iterationNote": "The isolated explicit Bedrock poly_mesh experiment at head 3528f1885a2713091a273eb0d5bb129cc9c351cd materialized and passed structural attachment, but Blockbench 5.1.6 silently omitted that mesh from the rendered scene. This pass therefore returns to the Bedrock cube primitive only as very thin cloth surfaces, cuts total head treatment to six pieces, removes the white chest-plate read, narrows the apron, and moves silhouette weight into blue side/rear drapes. This remains preview-only and is not a production-authored model."
  },
